WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:08.470
Hey, welcome back to 40 Days of Holiness.

2
00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:12.010
Grab your workbook, and if you don't have one, you know where you can get one,

3
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.040
40daysofholiness.com. You can get your workbook.

4
00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:18.099
Here's today's theme on day number seven.

5
00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:22.570
God taught us holiness throughout the Old Testament.

6
00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.460
So today, we're going to talk about the Book of Leviticus.

7
00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:29.360
Now, pretty much nobody says, "Yeah, man, I can't wait to read the

8
00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:32.580
Bible and the Book of Leviticus. It's going to be good times." Right?

9
00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:36.940
But Leviticus is a vital book for understanding what God

10
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.060
means when he says holiness. It's a primer on the subject.

11
00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:44.020
It's foundational, full of basic thoughts and definitions that are going to

12
00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:47.940
inform the rest of our education on the ideas of

13
00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:51.880
holiness and what it is. Now, people usually kind of run down Leviticus

14
00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:55.820
and call it boring or irrelevant or basically read it and say, "Oh, man, thank

15
00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:59.720
God we don't do all that stuff anymore, all those rules." Or they read it and

16
00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:03.220
say, "Well, most of these food laws were stuff about the health of the

17
00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:07.110
Israelites, and we need to do it so we can be healthy, too." And really, none of

18
00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:09.340
those perspectives is really fully accurate.

19
00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:13.950
A great portion of the purpose of the law, including Leviticus, is

20
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.690
not health or even to establish justice, though God cares about those things.

21
00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:21.940
The purpose of the Levitical law is to teach the children of Israel the

22
00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:25.980
holiness of God. Now, when Israel left Egypt, they were

23
00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:30.039
coming out of 400 years of regular exposure to the gods of

24
00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:34.040
Egypt, and these gods were very much like the people of Egypt that

25
00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:37.520
served them. They were capricious, immoral, untrustworthy,

26
00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:38.900
vengeful, and evil.

27
00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:43.380
Yet when you're slaves for a long period of time, you just start to

28
00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:47.120
automatically think that the gods of your masters must be more

29
00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:50.180
powerful than your gods. It's just human nature, like, "That's why we're here.

30
00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:54.160
God can't get us out of here." And God knew he was sending his people out of there

31
00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:57.960
to a land where a word for holy already existed.

32
00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:01.520
The word "kadesh" in Canaan, where they were headed, was already

33
00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:05.580
widespread in use. The priests of the pagan religions were called the

34
00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.840
kadesh or the kedeshim, the holy men.

35
00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:12.600
And if you translated kedeshim directly into English, it would come out

36
00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:15.840
as holy men or saints or something like that.

37
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:17.800
Some of them, what were these holy men?

38
00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:21.660
Well, they were male or female prostitutes who engaged in sexual activity as

39
00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:24.340
part of the worship of the religions of Canaan.

40
00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:29.109
The word "qdeshah," the holy woman, is used in Genesis 38 for one of those

41
00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:33.450
shrine prostitutes. They were immoral, and they weren't connected

42
00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:36.900
or like God, who's the source of all true holiness.

43
00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:41.250
They were men and women of a counterfeit holiness, and God would not

44
00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:45.290
allow his holiness, his name, to be maligned in the eyes

45
00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:48.880
of his people. So you see the problem God's going to head off here?

46
00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:52.920
Israel is going to arrive in Canaan, and they're going to come face to

47
00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:55.740
face with a culture where there are other gods.

48
00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:59.700
And somebody's going to ask the question, "What's your God like, this Yahweh?"

49
00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:03.400
And the children of Israel say, "Well, our God is holy." And the pagans say, "Oh,

50
00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:06.700
well, that's great. Our gods are holy, too.

51
00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:09.260
In fact, we have these holy men, these priests.

52
00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:12.760
Your God must be like our God. Let's worship together."

53
00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:17.490
And God is not okay with it. He says, "I will not share my

54
00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:21.030
glory, the glory of my holiness, with another." Remember,

55
00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:25.180
he's looking for exclusive, ongoing connection, a covenant

56
00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:29.090
relationship where his holiness, the essence of his nature,

57
00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:33.200
flows into those who are his. So God set about on

58
00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:37.040
this grand project to teach his people the meaning of

59
00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:41.120
his holiness. The giving of the law in the Old Testament revolves around

60
00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:45.080
the phrase, "For I am the Lord your God." It's often coupled with,

61
00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.680
"So you must be holy," or, "For I, the Lord your God, am holy." So the

62
00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:52.690
requirements of God flow out of the character of God, and

63
00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:55.120
the core of that character is holiness.

64
00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:59.300
And God was not okay with his people misunderstanding

65
00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:03.450
the nature of his holiness. And I think we need to stop here and mull this over for

66
00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:03.820
a second.

67
00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:07.660
I don't know what you've been taught about holiness in the past.

68
00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:11.700
Maybe you've been raised with an idea that said, "I can be

69
00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:13.880
a holy man and still be engaged in sin.

70
00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:17.820
I can deliberately, rebelliously do whatever I want and still be a holy man because

71
00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:21.260
I go to church on Sunday or I'm a member of the church." Maybe you were raised in a

72
00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:24.040
teaching that said holiness is a list of do's and don'ts.

73
00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:27.800
It's like a really difficult life where we have to strip away everything that

74
00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:31.600
brings joy and kind of live a hermit sort of life, and that's holiness.

75
00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:34.040
I want to encourage you with this.

76
00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:38.800
God is waiting and ready to teach you

77
00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:40.230
about his character.

78
00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:44.840
And we know he's committed to this because he decided you were going to be

79
00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:47.360
holy before the foundation of the world.

80
00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:51.140
And we can see the patience that he exercised with his people

81
00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:53.960
during his teaching process. They blew it.

82
00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:57.920
He was faithful still to them. He disciplined them.

83
00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:00.620
He sent them prophets. He sent them necessary pain.

84
00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:04.540
Sometimes he would send them rich blessing, and all of it was in the

85
00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:08.520
service of teaching them what it meant to be holy like he

86
00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:08.800
was.

87
00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:13.740
So as we pray today, let's put aside old

88
00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:17.440
definitions, old assumptions, old ignorance, maybe some past

89
00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:18.900
misunderstanding and hurt,

90
00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:23.710
and let's just open our minds to the great teacher, the one who does not

91
00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:26.920
want his name and his character to be misunderstood.

92
00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:30.790
He's more motivated to teach you than you are to learn about him.

93
00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:35.430
He wants to teach you what it means that he is holy and how he can

94
00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:38.220
produce that in your life.

95
00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:41.980
So today, while you're going through the workbook,

96
00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:45.780
just let it be glad that God was so patient.

97
00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:45.920
He

98
00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:50.560
endeavored to take an entire hundreds of years to teach his

99
00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:54.560
children, his people, the children of Israel, what it meant that he was

100
00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:57.580
holy, and he's going to teach you, too. All right?

101
00:05:57.940 --> 00:05:59.460
You be patient. You keep learning.

102
00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:03.240
You keep meditating, and God's going to reform and transform your mind

103
00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:06.640
until you understand exactly what he wants you to be like. All right?

104
00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:08.500
And what it is to be like him.

105
00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:11.300
I'll see you tomorrow. Thanks for being here.
